.TH MUTOS_LD 1 "2026-08-07" "MUTOS 1700 Toolchain" "MUTOS 1700 Cross-Compiler Toolchain"
.SH NAME
mutos_ld \- cross link editor for MUTOS 1700 / Unix V7 object files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_ld
[
.B \-o
.I output
]
[
.B \-u
.I symbol
]
[
.B \-e
.I symbol
]
[
.B \-D
.I number
]
[
.BR \-x | \-X | \-S | \-r | \-s | \-n | \-d | \-i | \-O | \-t
]
.I file
\&.\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_ld
combines one or more MUTOS 1700
.RI ( a.out
format) object files and archive libraries into a single executable or
relocatable output file. It is a cross-linker: it runs on a 64-bit Linux
host but reads and writes the original 16-bit little-endian MUTOS 1700
.I a.out
format used on the Robotron A7100/A7150 (an x86-16, 8086/V30-based system
that is binary- and architecture-compatible with Unix Version 7).
.PP
.B mutos_ld
is a faithful C11 port of the original MUTOS/Unix V7
.IR ld (1)
source. Its command-line syntax, two-pass symbol-resolution algorithm,
common-symbol allocation, and archive/library handling (including
.B __.SYMDEF
ranlib tables of contents) match the original exactly. The only
PDP-11-specific implementation detail that has been replaced is the
original's double-buffered page-oriented I/O layer, which is not
observable from the command line or in the output produced.
.PP
If no
.B \-o
option is given, output is written to the file
.B a.out
in the current directory. If the output file already contains
executable output and the link is successful, its execute permission
bits are ordinarily meaningful to the shell; no special action is taken
by
.B mutos_ld
itself beyond creating the file.
.PP
The last file operand may be an archive library (see
.B \-l
below and
.B ARCHIVES
).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o \ output
Produce the linked output in the file
.I output
instead of the default
.BR a.out .
.TP
.BI \-u \ symbol
Enter
.I symbol
as undefined in the symbol table. This forces the loading of an
archive library member that defines it, even if nothing else in the
link references it directly. Useful for pulling in library routines
that are only reached indirectly (e.g. via another library routine).
.TP
.BI \-e \ symbol
Use
.I symbol
as the entry point of the output file instead of the address of the
first
.B text
location. As with
.BR \-u ,
this also enters
.I symbol
into the symbol table as undefined, causing it to be resolved (and
pulled from a library, if necessary) like any other undefined
reference.
.TP
.BI \-D \ number
Set the starting address of the
.B data
segment to
.IR number ,
which must be at least as large as the total size of the
.B data
segments of the input files. This can be used to leave room, for
example, for a
.B text
segment that will be grown later, or to force a specific data origin
expected by a downstream tool.
.TP
.BI \-l x
Search the archive library
.BI lib x .a
for members that resolve currently undefined symbols, first in the
current directory, then in
.BR /usr/lib .
May be repeated, and may be freely interleaved with object file
operands and other
.BR \-l
options; libraries are searched left to right, and (as in the
original) it is often necessary to list a library more than once, or
after all files that reference symbols it defines, since only the
members needed to satisfy still-undefined symbols at the time the
library is reached are pulled in. See
.B ARCHIVES
below.
.TP
.B \-x
Do not preserve local (non-external) symbols in the output symbol
table, only external symbols are kept. This is the default action of
.IR strip (1)
applied by the linker itself while writing the output.
.TP
.B \-X
Preserve local symbols in the output symbol table except for those
whose names begin with the letter
.BR L .
Such symbols are conventionally generated internally by compilers and
assemblers and are rarely of interest for later debugging.
.TP
.B \-S
Strip all line-number and other non-relocatable debugging-style local
symbols (specifically, any symbol whose type is
.B N_ABS
or lies outside the ordinary
.BR N_TEXT / N_DATA / N_BSS
range) from the output, while otherwise leaving the local/external
symbol table intact.
.TP
.B \-r
Produce relocatable output: relocation information is retained in the
output file so that it can itself be used as an input file to a later
.B mutos_ld
invocation. Implies that the link may be left with undefined external
symbols without this being treated as a fatal error (only a warning is
issued; see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS ).
Also implies
.BR \-d ,
and forces off
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
and
.BR \-O ,
regardless of whether they were given, since those options apply only
to final, non-relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress output of the symbol table entirely (implies
.BR \-x ).
.TP
.B \-n
Produce output with a separate, read-only, shareable
.B text
segment
.RB ( NMAGIC ).
The
.B data
segment origin is additionally rounded up to a MUTOS/x86 page-style
(017777-byte) boundary beyond the padded end of
.BR text .
.TP
.B \-d
Force common symbol allocation even when the output still contains
unresolved externals. Ordinarily, common (tentatively defined)
symbols are only assigned final
.B bss
storage once the link is fully resolved; this forces the allocation
step to run regardless. Automatically enabled by
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B \-i
Produce output with separate instruction and data
.RB ( I&D ,
.BR IMAGIC )
address spaces: the
.B data
segment address space starts fresh at address 0 rather than
immediately following
.BR text ,
even though both segments still occupy consecutive space in the output
.I file
itself.
.TP
.B \-O
Produce plain, unpadded, non-shareable output
.RB ( OMAGIC ).
Takes precedence over
.B \-n
and
.B \-i
if given together with either.
.TP
.B \-t
Trace mode: print the name of each input file (and, for archive
members, the enclosing archive and member name) to standard output as
it is processed. Intended for debugging link order and library
resolution.
.SH ARCHIVES
An archive library given via
.B \-l
(or named directly as a file operand) is recognized by its leading
archive magic number, and is handled in one of three ways, matching
the original algorithm exactly:
.IP 1. 4
.B Plain archive.
If the archive has no
.B __.SYMDEF
member (or a non-symdef member happens to be first), every member is
scanned in turn and pulled in if it defines a symbol currently needed.
.IP 2. 4
.B Up-to-date table of contents.
If the archive's first member is an
.B __.SYMDEF
table of contents (as produced by
.IR ranlib (1))
whose embedded timestamp is not older than the archive file's own
modification time, the table of contents is used to pull in only the
members that are actually needed, repeated to a fixed point so that
symbols newly required by one pulled-in member can themselves trigger
further members to be pulled in.
.IP 3. 4
.B Stale table of contents.
If the archive file has been modified more recently than the
timestamp recorded in its
.B __.SYMDEF
member (i.e. after the last
.IR ranlib (1)
run), a warning is issued and the linker falls back to a full linear
scan of the remaining members, exactly as case 1.
.PP
Within a single link, an archive member is pulled in at most once, and
only if, at the time it is examined, it defines at least one symbol
that is still undefined. Passing a library before the object files
that reference it may therefore fail to resolve those references; list
libraries after the files (or other libraries) that need them, or
repeat
.B \-l
as necessary.
.SH FILE FORMAT
Object and executable files are in the original MUTOS 1700
.I a.out
format: a 16-byte header of eight 16-bit little-endian words (magic,
text size, data size, bss size, symbol table size, entry point, an
unused/reserved word, and a no-relocation flag), followed by
.BR text ,
.BR data ,
optional text and data relocation tables, and an optional symbol
table. Unlike classic PDP-11 Unix V7, no word ever needs middle-endian
handling in this format, since every field here is a plain 16-bit
quantity; MUTOS's own 32-bit
.B long
values (as used elsewhere in the toolchain and in archive headers) do
use PDP-11 middle-endian byte order, but that does not apply to any
field of the object/executable header itself.
.PP
.B mutos_ld
always requires its input files to carry relocation information
(the header's no-relocation flag must be clear); input produced with
that flag set is rejected with
.BR "No relocation bits" .
.PP
The magic number of the output file is selected according to the
options given, in this order of precedence:
.B OMAGIC
.RB ( \-O ),
.B IMAGIC
.RB ( \-i ),
.B NMAGIC
.RB ( \-n ),
otherwise plain
.BR FMAGIC .
.PP
Independently of the selected magic number, MUTOS
.B mutos_ld
always pads the end of the
.B text
segment up to the next 8-byte boundary (filled with the byte pattern
.BR "EB FE" ,
the x86 one-byte "jump to self" trap instruction) before the
.B data
segment begins; if
.B \-n
or
.B \-i
was given, an additional rounding up to a 64-byte boundary is applied
on top of that, filled with zero bytes. This differs from stock Unix
V7, where such padding is entirely conditional on
.B \-n
/
.BR \-i .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics are written to standard error, prefixed with
.B ld:
and the name of the input file (and, for archive members, the
enclosing archive member name in parentheses) currently being
processed.
.PP
Warnings (such as an out-of-date archive table of contents, or
undefined symbols when
.B \-r
was given) do not stop the link and do not by themselves cause a
non-zero low-order exit status bit; fatal errors (such as
.BR "Premature EOF" ,
.BR "Bad format" ,
segment size overflow, or a symbol table overflow) abort the link
immediately.
.PP
If undefined external symbols remain at the end of the link and
.B \-r
was not given, each such symbol's name is printed under the heading
.B Undefined:
and bit 0 of the process exit status is set; the output file is still
written.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful link, no diagnostics.
.TP
.B 1
(bit 0 set) One or more undefined external symbols remained, or
another non-fatal condition (e.g. a segment-size overflow warning) was
reported.
.TP
.B 2
A fatal error occurred (bad or truncated input, out of memory, cannot
open/create a file, etc); execution is aborted immediately at the
point of the error rather than continuing to the end of the link.
.TP
.B 4
No file operands were given at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B mutos_ld \-o hello crt0.o hello.o \-lc
Link
.B hello.o
against the startup module
.B crt0.o
and the standard library
.B libc.a
(searched as
.B libc.a
in the current directory, then
.BR /usr/lib/libc.a ),
producing the executable
.BR hello .
.TP
.B mutos_ld \-i \-o hello crt0.o hello.o \-lc
As above, but build a separate I&D
.RB ( IMAGIC )
executable.
.TP
.B mutos_ld \-r \-o combined.o a.o b.o
Combine
.B a.o
and
.B b.o
into a single relocatable object file
.B combined.o
suitable as input to a later
.B mutos_ld
invocation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mutos_as (1),
.IR mutos_cc (1),
.IR mutos_aout.h (5)
.SH HISTORY
.B mutos_ld
is a cross-platform C11 port, for 64-bit Linux hosts, of the original
Unix Version 7
.IR ld (1)
as adapted for the MUTOS 1700 operating system on the Robotron
A7100/A7150. The porting work (Milestone 1 of the MUTOS 1700
cross-compiler toolchain project) preserves the original two-pass
algorithm and command-line interface exactly, replacing only the
PDP-11-specific double-buffered page I/O layer with straightforward
whole-file in-memory processing; several MUTOS-specific deviations
from stock V7 behavior (segment-local relative symbol values,
unconditional 8-byte text padding, an 11-bit rather than 12-bit
relocation symbol-index field, and PDP-11 middle-endian archive header
.B long
fields) were determined by comparison against object and archive files
produced by, and linked with, real MUTOS 1700 hardware/toolchain
output, and are reproduced bit-for-bit.
.SH BUGS
Overlay support
.RB ( \-v
in the original V7
.IR ld )
is not implemented; using it produces a diagnostic and aborts the
link, since in the original it is tightly coupled to argument
bookkeeping that has no counterpart in this port's scope.
